{"records":[{"id":"s3jtdhjdbs","title":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Protein import motor complex reacts to mitochondrial misfolding by reducing protein import and activating mitophagy'","description":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Mitochondrial import loss induces mitophagy without membrane depolarization'. We hypothesized that inhibition of mitochondrial protein import induced mitophagy independent of mitochondrial depolarization. This dataset contains raw Illumina sequencing data for all samples from a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen for mitophagy regulators. The genome-wide library is Brunello from Doench et al Nat Biotechnol. 2016. We found that the pre-sequence translocase-associated motor complex PAM controls mitophagy induction. Loss of PAM caused a loss in protein import and was sufficient to induce mitophagy without depolarization.\nThe samples have been sequenced on an Illumina NextSeq 500 in single end mode with 75+8 cycles. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-05T15:38:53.752Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3jtdhjdbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3jtdhjdbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Michaelis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melinda","last_name":"Brunstein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Süleyman","last_name":"Bozkurt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ludovico","last_name":"Alves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Wegner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manuel","last_name":"Kaulich","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Pohl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Münch","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Mitochondrion","Mitophagy","Genetic Screening","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tjx2nbm2x8","title":"Mitochondrial fumarate inhibits Parkin-mediated mitophagy. Su Jin Ham et al.","description":"Here, we explore the potential involvement of fumarate, a metabolite generated from the citric acid cycle, as a key regulator of PINK1-Parkin-mediated mitophagy. Fumarate engages in a process called succination, forming S-(2-succino) cysteine through a Michael-like reaction with protein cysteine residues in the cell. Our research demonstrates that this modification specifically targets the sulfhydryl group of cysteine 323 and 451 residues of human Parkin, leading to the inhibition of its mitochondrial localization and E3 ligase activity, thereby impeding PINK1-Parkin-mediated mitophagy. Notably, our investigation reveals that the succinatable cysteines in human Parkin are not conserved in invertebrates, including Drosophila. To assess the functional impact of Parkin succination, we generated Parkin knock-in flies with succinatable cysteines. These flies exhibited robust Parkinson's disease (PD)-related phenotypes when exposed to elevated fumarate levels. Collectively, our findings underscore the significance of fumarate as an endogenous regulator of PINK1-Parkin-mediated mitophagy, offering insights into the intricate interplay between mitochondrial metabolic activities and PD pathology. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-17T19:27:20.498Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tjx2nbm2x8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tjx2nbm2x8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jongkyeong","last_name":"Chung","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"RS-2024-00440824"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00y0zf565","name":"Institute for Basic Science","grant_id":"IBS-R008-D1"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"NRF-2022R1I1A1A0105543312"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"NRF-2021R1A2C1010577"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"RS-2023-00211029"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"NRF-2020R1A5A1018081 "}],"categories":["Parkinson's Disease","Post-Translational Modification","Mitophagy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z46cyfd8tz","title":"Parkin_mediated_mitophagy_is_induced_by_the_probiotics_Saccharomyces_boulardii_and_Lactococcus_lactis","description":"Dysfunctional mitochondria are a hallmark feature of neurodegenerative disorders, such as Parkinson’s\ndisease, and upregulation of Parkin-mediated mitophagy pathways serve to remove unhealthy\nmitochondria from cells. Probiotics have been used to alleviate symptoms of Parkinson’s disease and\nthere is evidence to suggest that they modulate mitochondrial pathways. In this study, we conducted a\nscreen of 49 probiotics and tested various stages of mitophagy to determine whether any of the\nprobiotics can upregulate mitophagy in cells sustaining mitochondrial damage. We found that two\nprobiotics, Saccharomyces boulardii and Lactococcus lactis upregulate mitochondrial Parkin recruitment,\nphospho-ubiquitination, and mitofusin degradation. Further, these strains increase lysosomal\ndegradation of dysfunctional mitochondria in the brain of Drosophila that were subjected to the\nmitochondrial toxin paraquat, and also improve longevity and motor function in this Drosophila model of\nParkinson’s disease. These effects occur due to exogenous soluble factors that are released by probiotics\nunder laboratory conditions. We identified methyl-isoquinoline-6-carboxylate as one such molecule,\nwhich upregulates mitochondrial parkin recruitment, phospho-ubiquitination, mitofusin degradation, and\nlysosomal degradation, while restoring motor function in paraquat-treated flies. These data suggest a\nnovel mechanism that probiotics use to stimulate mitophagy through a Parkin-dependent pathway that\ncould potentially be utilized to treat neurological diseases at an early stage.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-29T09:55:33.564Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z46cyfd8tz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z46cyfd8tz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Hawrysh","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitophagy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d5vw9sjcrv","title":"A target expression threshold dictates invader defense  and autoimmunity by CRISPR-Cas13","description":"Immune systems must recognize and clear foreign invaders without eliciting autoimmunity. CRISPR-Cas immune systems in prokaryotes manage this task by following two criteria: extensive guide:target complementarity and a defined target-flanking motif. Here we report an additional requirement for RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 systems: expression of the target transcript exceeding a threshold. This finding is based on targeting endogenous non-essential transcripts, which rarely elicited dormancy through collateral RNA degradation. Instead, eliciting dormancy required over-expressing targeted transcripts above a threshold. A genome-wide screen confirmed target expression levels as the principal determinant of cytotoxic autoimmunity and revealed that the threshold shifts with the guide:target pair. This expression threshold ensured defense against a lytic bacteriophage yet allowed tolerance of a targeted beneficial gene expressed from an invading plasmid. These findings establish target expression levels as a third criterion for immune defense by RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas systems, buffering against autoimmunity and distinguishing pathogenic and benign invaders.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-08T11:55:48.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d5vw9sjcrv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d5vw9sjcrv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elena","last_name":"Vialetto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanying","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Collins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katharina","last_name":"Wandera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lars","last_name":"Barquist","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chase","last_name":"Beisel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis","Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nnxh5d35nd","title":"Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen identified KLF11 as a druggable suppressor for sarcoma cancer stem cells","description":"The original, unprocessed data alongside the paper","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-31T01:39:45.722Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nnxh5d35nd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nnxh5d35nd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Le","last_name":"Qu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"66ms37y66x","title":"Data for: A genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen reveals that riboflavin regulates hydrogen peroxide entry into HAP1 cells  ","description":"CRISPR screen data for hydrogen peroxide mediated cell death","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-13T14:30:16.414Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/66ms37y66x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/66ms37y66x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kenneth","last_name":"Mark","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tamutenda","last_name":"Chidawanyika","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oxidative Stress"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b5x637vrsk","title":"Sestrin2","description":"Sestrin2 Alleviates Age-Related Hearing Loss Through Mediating PINK1/Parkin Related Mitophagy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-03T19:03:02.855Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b5x637vrsk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b5x637vrsk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Liwen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":140629,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":167,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":140629,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139932,"restricted":69,"embargoed":628},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2291},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1390},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":678},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":471},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":450},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":433},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":385},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":330},{"id":"https://ror.org/